Crystal clear now: every 30 min a DIFFERENT old stale record (08-27, 09-02, 09-04 02:47...) gets registered — distinct timestamps, distinct identities. This is the **sorted-hash 500-cap eviction churn**: 611 records, `sorted(known)[-500:]` evicts ~111 arbitrary hashes; each scan re-discovers evicted records as "new", re-adds them (re-sorting evicts others) — a rolling churn that drips one old record per拍 into the board. My #636 identity dedup can't stop it because each stale record has a unique identity and was never registered before.

  Root fix for #637 should target this churn: `_scan_gate_blocked`'s eviction. Options:
  - Evict by insertion order (FIFO) instead of sorted-hash: keep an ordered list, append new hashes, drop oldest. But hashes alone can't order by time... state could store (hash) list in insertion order: `seen` list, append new, truncate from front. Currently `sorted(known)[-500:]` — sorted alphabetically! That's the bug-ish design. With FIFO eviction, old stale records evicted once stay out?? No — FIFO: when >500, oldest (by insertion) dropped; but all 611 records are re-added... every scan re-adds missing ones at the END, so membership churns regardless. Hmm.

  Real issue: log has 611 records but state cap is 500 — cap < record count guarantees permanent churn no matter the eviction policy. Fixes:
  a. Raise cap above log size (e.g. 500 → 5000) — cheap, works until log grows past it.
  b. Only scan the log TAIL (e.g. records after the last-seen record position / or since last scan timestamp) — the log is append-only; state could store the last processed line offset/count. Records before watermark never re-scanned → no churn, no eviction issue. This is the structural fix: store `gate_seen_pos` = number of records processed; each scan only processes records[pos:]. But log rotation/rewrite would break offset... log is append-only, fine. Keep hash set as safety too.

